<?nvs
// docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12's decoders, answering octets rather than
// text, and ADR 0102's route capture decoded the same way.
//
// Percent-decoding is defined over *octets*, and a `string` is guaranteed-valid
// UTF-8 by construction (ADR 0009 § 1). So the two facts this file shows are
// the same fact seen twice: where the decoded octets can be anything, the
// answer is `bytes` and a caller wanting text writes `as string`; where they
// must be text — a capture binds to a declared `tainted string` parameter — the
// decode refuses instead, and there is nowhere in between.

// A class with one route, declared here rather than in a directory of its own:
// ADR 0077 § 5's table is built by the same scan `Core\Program::implementing<T>`
// uses, over the unit, so a single-file program has a table like any other.
class Pages
{
    #[Core\Route(path:"/pages/{slug}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Pages::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(string $slug): string
    {
        return $slug;
    }
}

// 1. `%ff%fe%fd` is three octets and is not text: no `string` answer could have
// carried it, which is why `decodeComponent` answers `bytes`. Sliced a byte at
// a time so the answer is read as the buffer it is rather than rendered.
bytes $octets = Core\Uri::decodeComponent('%ff%fe%fd');
echo 'decoded octets: ',
    Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::slice($octets, 0, 1)), ' ',
    Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::slice($octets, 1, 1)), ' ',
    Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::slice($octets, 2, 1)), "\n";

// 2. The same decoder reached through a query string. `na%6de` is the *name*
// `name`, decoded as text because a name is the array key the pair is filed
// under and octets outside UTF-8 in a key would break that same ADR 0009 § 1
// guarantee the moment a program iterated the array. The value has no such
// constraint, so it arrives as the octets themselves — identical to the buffer
// above, which is what the comparison asserts.
array<mixed> $query = Core\Uri::parseQuery('na%6de=%ff%fe%fd');
tainted bytes $value = $query['name'] as bytes;
if (Core\Bytes::compare($value, $octets) == 0) {
    echo 'query name and value decode as octets too', "\n";
}

// 3. A route capture, decoded once where it crosses into the program: the
// handler declares `string $slug`, so `%20` is a space by the time anything
// reads it and never a `%20` the program would have to decode a second time.
// `Core\Router::match` asks the table the same question the door asks of an
// arriving request — ADR 0102 § 1 — and answers the same `Core\Router\Match`,
// which is why one decode serves both. A capture whose escapes spell octets
// outside UTF-8 has no `tainted string` to become and throws instead; that is
// the boundary line 1 does not have.
?Core\Router\Match $matched = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, '/pages/hello%20world');
if ($matched != null) {
    echo 'capture: ', $matched->param('slug') as string, "\n";
}
